# 18. REALIZED FX GAIN/LOSS

Determine whether the existing architecture can safely support realized FX
differences.

A realized FX difference can occur when:

- an invoice is recorded at one exchange rate;
- settlement occurs at another exchange rate.

Example:

Invoice:

USD 1,000 @ 83.00 = INR 83,000

Payment:

USD 1,000 @ 84.00 = INR 84,000

Difference:

INR 1,000 FX loss/gain depending on the accounting direction.

If implementing realized FX:

- create proper FX gain/loss accounts;
- use the existing journal engine;
- use existing posting controls;
- do not create a separate FX ledger;
- preserve the original invoice and payment amounts;
- generate the FX difference through normal double-entry accounting;
- audit the resulting accounting event.

The exact journal direction must be derived from the transaction/accounting
context.

Do not hard-code an incorrect universal debit/credit rule.

